<div class="banner">
    <h1>Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson Identification Table</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td><b>Scenario:</b> All About My Body</td>
        <td><b>Theme:</b> Hats and Shoes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3">
            <p><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Identify keywords and phrases in texts. Reading: Identify simple story elements (e.g., characters, setting). Phonemic Awareness: Identify a spoken word's initial and final phoneme if these words have been previously encountered and are attached to a picture.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3">
            <p><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can locate and identify keywords related to body parts and clothing within sentences (e.g., identifying "head" and "hat" in a description). Can recognize simple story elements, such as characters and setting, in texts about body parts and clothing (e.g., identifying "the boy" and "his hat" in a story). Can identify the initial and final sounds with letter correspondence in body parts and clothing-related vocabulary (e.g., the /h/ and /d/ sounds and letters in "hand" or the /t/ and /z/ sounds in "toes" when shown images of a hand or toes).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 class="aoa-title">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p>Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts with a "Read and Show" activity. The teacher holds up flashcards with pictures of body parts (e.g., hand, head, foot, toes) and clothing (e.g., hat, shoes, shirt) and their corresponding words written clearly. The teacher points to a word, asks students to read it aloud, and then asks them to point to that body part on themselves or touch the clothing item on them (if wearing it) or on the flashcard. This revises vocabulary from Lesson 1 and connects to the theme and scenario, focusing on reading words.</p>
                <p><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models reading each word aloud clearly, pointing to the word on the flashcard. For "hat" and "hand," the teacher explicitly points to the initial letter 'H' while saying the /h/ sound. For "toes," the teacher points to 'T' for the /t/ sound. This models proper word recognition and introduces phonemic awareness with letter correspondence.</p>
                <p><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word is this?" (Teacher points to the word 'shoes' on a flashcard, students read chorally).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'hand' start with /h/ or /t/ sound?" (Students respond and point to the letter 'H').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p>5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About My Body"</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful picture storybook or a printed story on a poster board with simple, illustrated sentences about two children, Tom and Ana, describing their body parts and clothing. The text uses Present Simple ("This is..."), Imperatives ("Put on..."), WH-questions ("Where is...?"), and target vocabulary.</p>
                <p><b>Example Text:</b></p>
                <p>"This is Tom. He has a red hat on his head. These are his blue shoes. They are on his feet. He has two hands and two legs. Put on your jacket, Tom!</p>
                <p>This is Ana. She has a green shirt. Where is her belt? Her belt is on her pants. These are her fingers. Touch your nose, Ana!"</p>
                <p><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpreting simple written messages about descriptions and instructions.</p>
                <p><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> The teacher reads the story aloud once, pointing to the words. Then, students are given a worksheet with simple reading comprehension tasks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Read and Circle):</b> Students read simple questions about the story and circle "Yes" or "No" (e.g., "Tom has a red hat. Yes / No." "Ana has a blue shirt. Yes / No.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching (Read and Match):</b> Students read phrases (e.g., "Tom's hat," "Ana's shirt") and draw a line to the correct corresponding picture from the story.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p>10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p>Accuracy focus on Reading</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice reading and understanding sentences with target grammar (Present Simple, Imperatives, WH-questions) and vocabulary (nouns: head, hand, hat, shoes, shirt, pants; verbs: put on, touch; possessive adjectives: my, your, his, her; prepositions: on).</p>
                <p><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Read and Circle (Controlled practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the story, but with a missing word and two choices. They read the sentence and circle the correct vocabulary word. (e.g., "Tom has a red (hat / hand) on his head." "Ana has a green (shirt / shoes)."). This activity reinforces keyword identification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Sentence Match (Semi-controlled practice):</b> Students are given a set of simple sentences on strips of paper (e.g., "This is my hand." "Put on your shoes." "Where is his hat?") and another set of pictures. They read each sentence strip and match it to the correct picture. This helps with reading simple story elements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Phoneme Search (Peer Check):</b> In pairs, students receive a short paragraph (a simplified version of the story text). One student reads a sentence. The other student identifies and points to words that start with the /h/ sound (e.g., "hat," "head," "hand") or the /t/ sound (e.g., "Tom," "toes," "touch"). They then swap roles. This activity integrates phonemic awareness with letter correspondence in a reading context.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p>12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About My Body"</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p><b>Task format:</b> "Read and Follow Directions" / "Read and Draw"</p>
                <p><b>Procedure:</b> Students work individually on a worksheet. Each worksheet has a simple outline of a person and several numbered instructions or descriptions to read. Students must read each sentence carefully and draw the described clothing item or body part onto the figure.</p>
                <p><b>Example Instructions:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw a boy with a hat on his head."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"His shoes are blue. Draw them on his feet."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Draw his two hands."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Put a shirt on him."</li>
                </ol>
                <p>This task requires students to read and interpret simple sentences, identify keywords related to body parts and clothing, and recognize simple story elements (the character being described) to complete the drawing. The teacher circulates, observing students' reading comprehension and offering support if needed.</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p>8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p>Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and a short written comprehension task.</p>
                <p><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a short reading passage (2-3 sentences, similar to the presentation text but new). Below the passage, there are three tasks:</p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Reading Comprehension:</b> Students read a simple 'Yes/No' question or a multiple-choice question about the text and circle the correct answer. (e.g., "This is a girl. She has a big hat. Her hat is red. She has small shoes. They are yellow." Question: "Is her hat red? Yes / No.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Keyword Identification:</b> Students read the passage again and underline all the words that are body parts or clothing items.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> Students are asked to identify a word from the text that starts with a specific sound, such as /h/ for "hat" or /s/ for "shoes." They write the word next to the sound.</li>
                </ol>
                <p><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in answering reading comprehension questions (identifying simple story elements).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to locate and identify keywords related to body parts and clothing.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of initial sounds with letter correspondence in target vocabulary.</li>
                </ul>
                <p><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small paper slip before leaving:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I can read new words like __________ and __________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Something I want to practice reading is __________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p>7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td>
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b> The teacher asks students to think about their reading today:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading the story or words was easy for you?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging when you were reading?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., Students effectively identified keywords in the "Read and Circle" activity, or showed good comprehension in the "Read and Draw" task.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., More visual support for new vocabulary, more explicit practice of phoneme-grapheme correspondence, shorter texts for some students.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How did the integration of phonemic awareness activities support reading comprehension for this age group?</li>
                </ul>
                <p><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading and understanding vocabulary related to body parts and clothing. In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will actively use this vocabulary and grammar in spoken interactions and simple dialogues, describing body parts and clothing, and following verbal instructions, building on the reading comprehension established today.</p>
            </td>
            <td>
                <p>3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>